You can't structurally modify an `ArrayList` while a for-each loop is iterating over it. The enhanced `for` loop uses an `Iterator` behind the scenes, and `ArrayList` keeps an internal modification counter (`modCount`). Calling `list.remove(...)` directly changes that counter, and the iterator's next call to `next()` notices the mismatch and throws `ConcurrentModificationException`. This is called *fail-fast* behaviour.

Use one of these instead:

**1. `removeIf` (simplest, Java 8+)**
```java
List<String> orders = new ArrayList<>(List.of("paid", "cancelled", "shipped", "cancelled"));
orders.removeIf(status -> status.equals("cancelled"));
System.out.println(orders); // [paid, shipped]
```

**2. An explicit `Iterator` and its own `remove()`**
```java
Iterator<String> it = orders.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    if (it.next().equals("cancelled")) {
        it.remove(); // safe: the iterator keeps its expected count in sync
    }
}
```

Note: removing the second-to-last element sometimes does *not* throw, because `hasNext()` returns `false` before `next()` gets a chance to check. Don't rely on that; it's still a bug.

Use a **sealed interface** with **record** implementations, then switch over it with **pattern matching for switch** (final in Java 21). Because the hierarchy is sealed, the compiler knows every permitted subtype, so a switch without `default` must cover them all. If you add a new payment type later, every switch that doesn't handle it becomes a compile error.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

sealed interface Payment permits Card, BankTransfer, Wallet {}
record Card(String last4, boolean international) implements Payment {}
record BankTransfer(String iban) implements Payment {}
record Wallet(String provider) implements Payment {}

public class FeeCalculator {
    static BigDecimal fee(Payment p, BigDecimal amount) {
        return switch (p) {
            case Card c when c.international() -> amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.035"));
            case Card c -> amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.015"));
            case BankTransfer b -> new BigDecimal("0.50");
            case Wallet(String provider) -> provider.equals("internal")
                    ? BigDecimal.ZERO
                    : amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.02"));
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(fee(new Card("4242", true), new BigDecimal("100")));  // 3.500
        System.out.println(fee(new Wallet("internal"), new BigDecimal("100"))); // 0
    }
}
```

Key points:
- `case Card c when ...` is a **guarded pattern**. It must come before the unguarded `case Card c`; otherwise the compiler reports that it is dominated by the earlier label.
- `case Wallet(String provider)` is a **record pattern** that deconstructs the record directly.
- There is no `default` branch on purpose: that is what keeps the exhaustiveness check useful.
- Records are implicitly `final`, which satisfies the rule that permitted subtypes be `final`, `sealed` or `non-sealed`.

On Java 17 you can already use sealed types and records, but pattern matching for switch was still a preview feature there.

No. `volatile` guarantees **visibility** and ordering (every read sees the most recent write), but it does not make compound actions **atomic**. `count++` is three steps: read `count`, add 1, write it back. Two threads can both read 41 and both write 42, losing an increment. Under load the final count ends up lower than the number of calls.

Make the increment atomic instead:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

class RequestCounter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger();

    void hit() { count.incrementAndGet(); }  // atomic, CAS-based
    int current() { return count.get(); }
}
```
Alternatively, guard both the increment and the read with `synchronized`. `volatile` on its own is enough only when a single thread writes and others read (a `running` flag, for example), or when a new value never depends on the previous one.

Yes: `java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder`. With `AtomicInteger`, every thread performs compare-and-set on the **same** memory location. Under heavy contention most CAS attempts fail and retry, and the cache line keeps bouncing between cores.

`LongAdder` spreads updates across an internal array of cells, so threads usually update different cells and rarely collide. Reading the value with `sum()` adds the base value and all the cells together.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;

class RequestCounter {
    private final LongAdder count = new LongAdder();

    void hit() { count.increment(); }
    long current() { return count.sum(); }
}
```

Trade-offs:
- `sum()` is **not an atomic snapshot**. Increments that happen during the call may or may not be included. That is fine for metrics, but not for generating unique IDs.
- There is no `incrementAndGet()`. If you need the new value back atomically (sequence numbers, for example), stay with `AtomicLong`.
- It uses more memory (extra padded cells under contention, bounded by the number of CPUs), so it isn't worth it for counters with little contention.

Rule of thumb: **heavy concurrent writes with occasional reads → `LongAdder`; an exact atomic read-modify-write → `AtomicLong` or `AtomicInteger`.**

Is this XML parsing code safe? It parses files that users upload.
```java
DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
DocumentBuilder db = dbf.newDocumentBuilder();
Document doc = db.parse(uploadedFile);
```

No. It is vulnerable to **XML External Entity (XXE)** injection (CWE-611). A default `DocumentBuilderFactory` processes DTDs, so an attacker can upload XML that declares an external entity pointing at a local file or an internal URL:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!DOCTYPE data [ <!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd"> ]>
<data>&xxe;</data>
```

When the parser expands `&xxe;`, the file's contents land in your document and possibly in a response. `http://` entities turn this into SSRF against internal services, and nested entity expansion ("billion laughs") can be used for denial of service.

**Fix: disallow DTDs entirely** (the OWASP-recommended setting), plus defense in depth:

```java
// FIXED
DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
dbf.setFeature("http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl", true);
dbf.setFeature("http://xml.org/sax/features/external-general-entities", false);
dbf.setFeature("http://xml.org/sax/features/external-parameter-entities", false);
dbf.setFeature("http://apache.org/xml/features/nonvalidating/load-external-dtd", false);
dbf.setXIncludeAware(false);
dbf.setExpandEntityReferences(false);

DocumentBuilder db = dbf.newDocumentBuilder();
Document doc = db.parse(uploadedFile); // any DOCTYPE now causes a SAXParseException
```

Notes:
- If you genuinely need DTDs, leave `disallow-doctype-decl` off but keep the external-entity features disabled, and set `dbf.setAttribute(XMLConstants.ACCESS_EXTERNAL_DTD, "")` and `ACCESS_EXTERNAL_SCHEMA` to `""`.
- Harden every XML entry point, not just this one: `SAXParserFactory`, `XMLInputFactory` (StAX), `TransformerFactory` and `SchemaFactory` each have their own settings.
- Don't rely on `FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING` alone. What it blocks varies between parser implementations and versions.
- Lock it in with a unit test that parses the payload above and asserts a `SAXParseException`.

